From: u01kdg@aberdeen.ac.uk
Date: Tue, 6 Dec 1994 11:36:48 +0000 (GMT)
Subject: [#94-12-213] Changing Viewed Spectra

I suppose that I've read enough to get the gist of what extropianism is all
about, but haven't yet read much literature-tolerate this please.
	My idea stems from what I just read about up-grading the Brain, and
falls into two main points:

1) The idea that the Brain, if upgraded, may suffer what might be called
'application' or 'syntax errors', is too simplistic a view of the Brain
(IMHO).Although I have really not read much on the cognitive and psychological
behaviour of a patient who has had his brain augmented, I feel that the
plasticity of the mind allows a great deal of alteration and addition to take
place, without detrimental effect.I would suggest though, that a period of
mind-physiotherapy would have to follow such operations so that the patient may
learn how to fully use his new device whilst having no previous knowledge of how
to, or even intuitive grasp of what should be done.By way of analogy, think of
the amputee, who must train his musculature in the shoulder/stump region to
switch actuators on and off in the artifical hand, allowing meaningful
movement.Not a brilliant analogy I know, but it is only 9 am.

2)The comment on changing the EM spectrum seen by the human eye, and changing
it, reminds me of an idea that (I) once had on how this might be achieved.The
actual conversion of external light to a nervous signal which may be converted
by the optic cortex, so that sight is possible, occurs at the level of the
retina with a chemical change to the chemistry of Rods and Cones.If I remember
correctly, the chemical involved is Rhodopsin.Now, Rhodopsin reacts to a range
of wavelengths of light in the 'visible' spectrum, allowing us to see in a
conventional sense.
	What if we were able to change this chemical, or alter rhodopsin so that
it reacted to light from a higher/lower wavelength?I postulate that we would
'see' light in the infrared/ultraviolet spectrum, but this might cause
difficulties at the level of the optic cortex-once again, a period of retraining
might follow before the human could make full use of his new range of vision.
	The practicalities of such an exercise might defeat us though.Dietary
manipulation of Rhosopsin precursors might allow us a simple means of altering
its reaction to visible light.Genetic engineering of retinal cells may also be
of use; perhaps via a retroviral vector innoculated directly into the humors of
the eye.
	Who knows?Could this work?

Da'Claw


