Vince Kerchner wrote:

>As far as the billionfold speedup is concerned, let's do a few simple
>calculations.  Assume a very simple NN program for which
>
>  output =3D threshold_function(sum(inputs))
>
>for each neuron.  Assume about 1e11 neurons, each with connections to about
>1e4 other neurons (a rather small "brain").  Assume a desired timescale of
>1e-3 seconds per calculation cycle (a not very "aware" program).  This
>program requires 1e15 adds and 1e11 thresholds per cycle, 1e3 times a
>second.  This requires
>
>  1e15 * 1e3 =3D 1e18 floating-point operations per second, or
>               1000 petaflops, or
>               1 million teraflops, or
>               1 billion gigaflops, or
>               1 trillion megaflops
>
>Given that today's computers are measured in Mflops, this sounds like a
>billionfold *slowdown* to me.  Yes, you would want to do this in parallel
>rather than serial form, but then you have to create a machine with 1e11
>elements, each of which is connected to another 1e4 elements.  I doubt VME,
>Multi, Nu, or any other bus design could handle this, Von Neuman
>architecture or not.

Yes, brute-force uploads require huge amounts of computing power (this one
reason I think computing needs of the VR environments they will live in
are of no importance, compared to their inhabitants they are nothing more
than screen-savers). However, computing speed is going up (I think we are
close to supercomputers with a teraflop capacity if I don't misremember),
there are lots of promise of further advances in technology on this
timescale, and human brains are quite obviously paralellizable (much
easier to paralellize than artificial neural networks). 

We know that a processing system with sufficient power exists. What we 
need is just a way to build it and transfer the information there (one 
possible approach might be to find a way to build an improved brain 
somehow, perhaps based on a combination of genetic engineering and 
conventional electronics). 


>Probably the best bet is to grow the system from scratch out of metal
>crystal, roughly the same way the biological system is formed (but
>hopefully much faster).  But then, training and/or transfer would be a
>problem, since you would have to be connected to the incipient system while
>the new NN was formed, guided by your own neural pathways.  This process
>would not be amenable to the "replace one neuron at a time" strategy
>already espoused.

There are lots of different strategies. I like the "replace one neuron at 
a time" strategy, because its elegant and circumvents some of the common 
philosophical objections (or at least confuses them :-). However, 
destructive scanning (especially of cryonically frozen brains) is much 
more probable. Non-destructive scanning is interesting, but its hard to 
tell if it will be possible. 

>>I think you put too much emphasis on raw horsepower. Even a
>>small amount of energy can do amazing things if guided by enough
>>information.
>
>'scuse me?  Although I am familiar with E =3D M*C^2 =3D H*k*T*ln(2) and all
>that, just how do you propose to get a rock into orbit by computation
>alone?  Any way you cut it, you still need M*G*H Joules.  An answer like
>"with infinite computational resources we can figure out a way to
>[whatever]..." isn't very satisfactory.  Specifics, please?

I would disassemble the rock, send the information to assemblers in orbit 
and let them build the rock. Actually transferring the rock up to orbit 
is expensive and unnecessary. 

>Reilly Jones <70544.1227@compuserve.com> sez:
>>On a cross-thread issue, you are being such a wet blanket about the
>>singularity, nanotech, rapture of the future, etc., even to the point of
>>invoking the dread term "mysticism."
>
>mys*ti*cism (m=BEs2t=BE-s=BEz1=F5m) n.
>1.a. Immediate consciousness of the transcendent or ultimate reality or
>God. b. The experience of such communion as described by mystics.

Small side track: Has anybody yet experienced the communion with the Omega
Point?  Don't worry, if somebody haven't already done it, somebody will! :-)

>However, this is an often glossed-over issue concerning uploading: the
>"blind" copying of neural architecture without regard for design or
>understanding.  While we may end up with faster perception and cognition,
>we'll also end up with faster neurosis, epilepsy, depression,
>schizophrenia, and a host of other physiological and psychological
>disorders.  I'd much rather know enough about how the brain and mind work
>to leave all that behind.

This is important (as discussed in the upgrading thread). We must find 
both ways to encode the human brain more efficiently in a computer (do we 
really need to store the electric data of each neuron, or can they be 
chunked to functional units?), and to prepare ourselves 
psychologically before uploading. Of course, uploaded brains may be 
easier to cure from lots of problems (epilepsy could be controlled using 
chaos theory, depression combatted by finding the relevant parts and 
influencing them etc). 

>Yes.  Coping with the myriad augmented-mental equivalents of bus and
>address errors (e.g.) will be one of those intermediate-step "details" that
>are usually left out of this discussion.

Fortunately the human brain manages to handle them. Ever thought of the 
number of neurons which misfire just because you are sitting close to a 
terminal or moving?

