Who said anything about uploads being required?  That seems to be just an 
assumption you drew out of thin air. 

What physics are you refering to?  If you could do as much thinking in a 
microsecond as someone else could do in a second, you think you wouldn't notice
things that escape the slower thinker?  You think you wouldn't be able to come 
up with new discoveries in physics that might change your view of what is 
possible?  After only a few centuries of this slower type of thinking, and the
applications of those slow-thought discoveries in physics made only in the last 
100 years being applied in computers and lasers among other things creating 
change at an accelerating rate, your statement is either a product of 
extraordinary arrogance or a nonexistent imagination.    

>Not until we can think and act on a scale which is comparable to Planck's
>constant (6.63E-34 J-sec) will there be any non-linear change in our
>perceived universe, and before we reach that point there will be *lots* of
>other difficulties to surpass.  Mass will still be mass, energy will still
>be energy, and all the stuff that's difficult for us now will still be
>difficult for us then.  And please don't bring into the discussion that
>holy panacea of Nanotech: just call me when it happens.

I  have yet to hear any who believe that nanotech is possible claim that it is a
panacea; quite the opposite.  As Drexler and others have pointed out, while it 
will bring a host of new possibilities for what most people would call "good", 
there are also some possibilities for some very big problems as well.  Besides, 
I don't think it's necessary for the singularity to start.  Though obviously if 
such a thing does occur, nanotech will be easy not long after that.  There is still a
lot that can be done with microtech yet.

I think that perhaps Perry and also Hans Moravec have placed too much emphasis 
on speed as a measure of intelligence; do we really think faster than chimps 
or just better?  If you could attach a number to the relative difference in 
speed of thought between  a chimp and a human, would you say that number is 
close to 6.6e34 times faster?   If so, I'd be interested in your reasoning; if 
not, your contention that we would need to be able to think and act on the scale 
of Planck's constant to notice nonlinear changes is false.  Things that are 
difficult for a chimp - such as saying hello - are easy for most humans.  That 
leaves out all the things that humans can learn to do that are impossible for a
chimp, such as playing the piano or solving calculus problems and a host of 
other things that are beyond its possibility of imagining.  All this occured 
through the process of "natural" evolution; e.g. before the prospect of being 
able to take a hand in our own evolution became imaginable.  Speed is certainly
important, but the cerrebocortex is small in proportion to the rest of the brain 
by weight, so it seems likely that intelligence has more to do with how neurons 
are organized and the number of connections between them and how fast these
connections can change than on sheer speed.  Though a simple speedup in nerve
activity I think would yield extraordinary results so long as it were done 
properly.  William Kelvin wrote a short essay about the transition to the 
superhuman in which he mentions the possible psychological problems associated 
with too rapid a change of mental capability.  Still, if you are thinking 
stupidly or just inefficiently, speeding up that process will probably just 
yield more stupid and inefficient thinking.  On the other hand, what might even 
a mediocre physicist or computer scientist be able to accomplish with a 
thousanfold speedup in thought; let alone Einstein or von Neumann?    

>It's my understanding that the so-called "singularity" will appear so only
>to those who do not avail themselves of advancing technology.  There are
>civilizations on this planet which have not changed much in the past 1,000+
>years, while we in the West have marched on with technology.  I doubt that
